Lab 1: Introduction to Vagrant and Shell Scripting
Lab Objective:
This lab is designed to introduce you to the basics of Vagrant and Shell scripting concepts. You will learn how to set up a basic Vagrant environment, write shell scripts to automate tasks, and run these scripts on a Vagrant-managed machine.

Prerequisites:
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine. Ensure that you have a working Ubuntu machine with proper administrative rights.

Install Vagrant and VirtualBox on your machine:

Ensure that you have Vagrant installed on the machine.

Install VirtualBox as it is required for Vagrant to manage virtual machines. VirtualBox is an open-source tool for creating virtual environments.

Install VirtualBox:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install virtualbox
Install Vagrant:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install vagrant
Lab Tasks:
1. Set up a basic Vagrant environment:

Open your terminal.

Create a new directory for your Vagrant environment:

mkdir vagrant_lab
cd vagrant_lab
Initialize a basic Vagrant environment:

vagrant init
This will create a Vagrantfile in the directory. The file will have default settings for your virtual machine.

Open the Vagrantfile and ensure it is set to use Ubuntu. You can modify the file to set the box to ubuntu/bionic64 (Ubuntu 18.04). You should see a line like this:

config.vm.box = "ubuntu/bionic64"
Now, run the following command to start the Vagrant virtual machine:

vagrant up
To connect to your virtual machine:

vagrant ssh
2. Write a shell script to display system information:

Once you are inside the Vagrant virtual machine, create a simple shell script to display system information:

nano system_info.sh
Add the following code to the file:

#!/bin/bash
echo "System Information:"
echo "-------------------"
uname -a
echo ""
echo "Disk Usage:"
df -h
echo ""
echo "Memory Usage:"
free -h
Save the file by pressing CTRL + O and exit by pressing CTRL + X.

Make the script executable:

chmod +x system_info.sh
Run the script to see the system information:

./system_info.sh
3. Test and run scripts on the Vagrant machine:

To test the script on your Vagrant machine, simply run the following command within the SSH session of the Vagrant VM:

./system_info.sh
The script should output information about your system's architecture, disk usage, and memory usage.

Exit the Vagrant machine after testing:

exit
Conclusion:
In this lab, you successfully set up a Vagrant environment and learned how to create and run shell scripts. The Vagrant virtual machine provided a safe and isolated environment to practice automation and system management tasks using shell scripting. This foundational knowledge will be useful for automating administrative tasks and managing systems efficiently.

By completing this lab, you have:

Set up a virtual machine using Vagrant.

Written a shell script to display system information.

Run and tested the script in a Vagrant-managed environment.

These skills are critical for automating tasks and working with virtualized environments, forming the basis for more complex DevOps workflows.
